"compare-contractor-quotes-uae": {
"slug": "compare-contractor-quotes-uae",
"title": "How to compare contractor quotes in the UAE without getting burned",
"excerpt": "Three quotes that look similar can hide very different scopes. Here is how UAE buyers read past the bottom line.",
"meta_desc": "A practical guide to comparing contractor and supplier quotes in the UAE — scope, exclusions, payment terms, approvals and the traps that make the cheapest quote the most expensive.",
"body_html": "<p>The cheapest quote is rarely the cheapest project. In the UAE, where the same job can be priced by a one-man trade licence or a fully-staffed contractor, the number at the bottom of the page tells you almost nothing on its own. Here is how experienced buyers actually compare.</p>\n<h2>1. Compare scope before price</h2>\n<p>Line up every quote against the same checklist: what materials, what brand or grade, what quantity, and crucially <strong>what is excluded</strong>. A quote that excludes authority approvals, MEP works, making-good, or site cleaning can look 15% cheaper and end up more expensive once those gaps are filled by variation orders.</p>\n<h2>2. Read the exclusions, not just the inclusions</h2>\n<p>UAE quotations often bury the real story in an \"exclusions\" or \"clarifications\" section. Common ones to watch: VAT not included, Dubai Municipality / DDA / Civil Defence approval fees by client, removal of existing works, and warranty limited to \"manufacturing defects only\".</p>\n<h2>3. Check the specification, not the adjective</h2>\n<p>\"High quality\" means nothing. A railing quoted with 5mm glass is not comparable to one with 6mm tempered glass; \"powder coated\" is not the same as marine-grade powder coat. Ask every supplier to state the exact grade, thickness, standard (e.g. UL/FM, NFPA, ISO) and brand.</p>\n<h2>4. Weigh delivery against your real deadline</h2>\n<p>A four-week lead time is worth paying for if your handover is fixed; it is worth nothing if you have three months. Don't pay a premium for speed you don't need — but do confirm whether the timeline is from order, from advance payment, or from approval.</p>\n<h2>5. Understand the payment terms</h2>\n<p>Advance percentage, milestone payments, and retention all change the real cost of money. A 50% advance to a supplier you don't know is a different risk than 20% on delivery. In the UAE, retention of 5–10% held until the defects-liability period ends is normal for larger works.</p>\n<h2>6. Separate the price from the relationship</h2>\n<p>Track record matters: ask for two recent reference projects of similar size, and check the trade licence covers the activity you're buying. A supplier licensed for \"general maintenance\" quoting a full fit-out is a red flag.</p>\n<h2>The shortcut</h2>\n<p>This is exactly what QuotesDone automates. You describe the job once, matched suppliers quote against the <em>same</em> anonymised scope, and our comparison agent flags what each quote includes, what's missing versus your request, and where a spec deviates — so you compare like-for-like instead of guessing. You stay anonymous until you choose to reveal the supplier you want.</p>\n<p><a href=\"/app\">Get comparable quotes →</a></p>",
"author": "QuotesDone",
"category": "Buyer guides",
"published_at": "2026-06-26T08:34:15.277142+00:00",
