"fit-out-quote-dubai-checklist": {
"slug": "fit-out-quote-dubai-checklist",
"title": "What a fair office fit-out quote in Dubai should include",
"excerpt": "A proper fit-out quote is more than flooring and paint. Use this checklist before you sign anything.",
"meta_desc": "A checklist of what a complete office fit-out quotation in Dubai should cover — approvals, MEP, joinery, HVAC, fire compliance, defects liability — so you can spot underscoped quotes.",
"body_html": "<p>Office fit-out is where underscoped quotes do the most damage, because so much of the cost is invisible until the work starts. A complete Dubai fit-out quotation should address all of the following — if any are missing, ask why.</p>\n<h2>Authority approvals</h2>\n<p>Fit-out works in Dubai typically need landlord NOC and approval from the relevant authority — Dubai Municipality, DDA, Trakhees, DMCC or the free-zone authority depending on location, plus Civil Defence for anything touching fire or life-safety systems. Confirm whether approval drawings, fees and follow-up are in the contractor's scope or yours.</p>\n<h2>MEP works</h2>\n<p>Mechanical, electrical and plumbing is the single most common exclusion. Lighting, power points, data, HVAC modifications, drainage for a pantry or washroom — these should be itemised, not assumed.</p>\n<h2>HVAC and the chilled-water / FAHU interface</h2>\n<p>Re-balancing the AC after partitions change the airflow is real work. A quote that adds partitions but ignores HVAC re-balancing will leave you with hot and cold zones.</p>\n<h2>Fire and life safety</h2>\n<p>New partitions usually mean relocating smoke detectors, sprinkler heads and emergency lighting, plus Civil Defence sign-off. This must be in the quote and the programme.</p>\n<h2>Joinery and finishes</h2>\n<p>Specify materials precisely: laminate grade, ironmongery brand, paint type and number of coats, flooring type and skirting. \"As per design\" is not a specification.</p>\n<h2>Making good, cleaning and handover</h2>\n<p>Removal of existing works, protection of common areas, post-construction cleaning, and as-built drawings are legitimate costs. So is a snagging period.</p>\n<h2>Defects-liability period</h2>\n<p>A serious contractor offers a defects-liability period (commonly 12 months) and states retention terms. No warranty at all is a warning sign.</p>\n<h2>Programme and payment</h2>\n<p>A week-by-week programme tied to milestone payments protects both sides. Match the payment schedule to delivered milestones, not to the calendar.</p>\n<h2>Compare the whole scope, not the bottom line</h2>\n<p>On QuotesDone, a multi-trade job like a fit-out is consolidated to one accountable turnkey contractor rather than scattered across five trades — and every quote is compared against the same scope, with missing items and spec deviations flagged automatically.</p>\n<p><a href=\"/app\">Get fit-out quotes →</a></p>",
"author": "QuotesDone",
"category": "Buyer guides",
"published_at": "2026-06-26T08:34:15.277142+00:00",
"sort": 20
},
"fire-fighting-systems-uae-civil-defence": {
"slug": "fire-fighting-systems-uae-civil-defence",
"title": "Fire-fighting systems in the UAE: approvals, standards and what to ask suppliers",
"excerpt": "Civil Defence approval, NFPA standards and UL/FM listings decide whether your fire system passes. Here is what to specify.",
"meta_desc": "A buyer guide to procuring fire-fighting systems in the UAE — Civil Defence approval, NFPA 20/13 standards, UL/FM listed equipment, and the questions to ask every supplier.",
"body_html": "<p>Fire-fighting is one area where the cheapest quote can literally fail inspection. UAE projects are governed by the UAE Fire and Life Safety Code of Practice, enforced by Civil Defence, and the requirements are specific. Here's what to specify and ask.</p>\n<h2>Civil Defence approval is non-negotiable</h2>\n<p>Fire systems must be designed, installed and commissioned by Civil-Defence-approved contractors, with approved drawings and a final inspection before occupancy. Always confirm the supplier is an approved contractor and that approval drawings and sign-off are in scope.</p>\n<h2>Know the standards that apply</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>NFPA 20</strong> — installation of stationary fire pumps.</li>\n<li><strong>NFPA 13</strong> — sprinkler system design.</li>\n<li><strong>NFPA 14</strong> — standpipe and hose systems.</li>\n<li><strong>NFPA 72</strong> — fire alarm and detection.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A quote should state which standards it is built to, not just \"as per Civil Defence\".</p>\n<h2>Insist on UL/FM listed equipment</h2>\n<p>Pumps, valves and key components should be UL listed and/or FM approved. \"UL listed, FM pending\" is not the same as FM approved — and that gap can stall your inspection. Make the listing an explicit line item.</p>\n<h2>Match the duty to the building</h2>\n<p>Pump flow (GPM) and pressure (bar), tank capacity, and standby/jockey pump arrangement must match the building's hydraulic calculation — not a generic figure. Ask for the basis of the sizing.</p>\n<h2>Commissioning, AMC and spares</h2>\n<p>Commissioning, witnessed testing, and a maintenance contract (AMC) keep the system compliant after handover. A quote that includes commissioning and a defined warranty is worth more than a bare supply price.</p>\n<h2>Ask the right routing question</h2>\n<p>A fire-pump enquiry should go to fire-fighting specialists — not a general MEP or steel-pipe supplier. On QuotesDone, the matching engine routes by actual capability, so a pump enquiry reaches genuine fire-fighting suppliers and never lands as spam in an unrelated trade's inbox.</p>\n<p><a href=\"/app\">Get fire-system quotes →</a></p>",
"author": "QuotesDone",
"category": "Trade guides",
"published_at": "2026-06-26T08:34:15.277142+00:00",
"sort": 30
