"how-much-does-cctv-installation-cost-in-the-uae-2026-price-guide": {
"slug": "how-much-does-cctv-installation-cost-in-the-uae-2026-price-guide",
"title": "How much does CCTV installation cost in the UAE? (2026 price guide)",
"excerpt": "A clear 2026 breakdown of CCTV installation costs in the UAE, including per-camera pricing, SIRA compliance, and what drives your final quote.",
"meta_desc": "CCTV installation costs in the UAE for 2026: per-camera prices, IP vs analog, DVR/NVR, SIRA rules and how to compare quotes without overpaying.",
"body_html": "<p>CCTV is one of the most common security upgrades UAE businesses ask about, yet quotes vary wildly. One supplier says AED 1,500, another says AED 6,000 for what looks like the same job. This 2026 guide explains what actually drives CCTV installation cost in the UAE, so you can budget accurately and compare like-for-like.</p><h2>Typical CCTV installation costs in the UAE (2026)</h2><p>Pricing depends on camera type, resolution, number of cameras, cabling distance, and whether you need a SIRA-compliant setup. As indicative ranges for a supply-and-install job:</p><ul><li><strong>Per camera (2MP HD analog):</strong> AED 250 - AED 600 installed</li><li><strong>Per camera (4MP-8MP IP):</strong> AED 600 - AED 1,500 installed</li><li><strong>4-camera package (basic HD):</strong> AED 1,200 - AED 2,500</li><li><strong>8-camera package (IP, NVR, remote view):</strong> AED 3,500 - AED 7,500</li><li><strong>16-camera commercial system:</strong> AED 8,000 - AED 20,000+</li></ul><p>These are guidance figures only. Actual quotes shift with brand, site conditions and compliance requirements. Always confirm the exact scope before comparing numbers.</p><h3>What's usually included</h3><ul><li>Cameras and mounting brackets</li><li>DVR (analog) or NVR (IP) recorder with a hard drive</li><li>Cabling, conduit and connectors</li><li>Power supply and network configuration</li><li>Mobile app setup for remote viewing</li></ul><h2>What drives the price up or down</h2><h3>1. Camera type and resolution</h3><p>Analog HD cameras are cheaper but lower quality. IP cameras give sharper footage, better night vision and features like motion analytics, but they cost more and often need a network switch. Higher resolution (4MP, 8MP) also increases storage requirements, which affects the recorder and hard drive size.</p><h3>2. Recording and storage</h3><p>A larger hard drive stores more days of footage before overwriting. Many businesses want 30 days of retention, which pushes storage cost. Some sites also add cloud backup or a second recorder for redundancy.</p><h3>3. Cabling and site conditions</h3><p>Long cable runs, ceiling-void work, external routing and drilling through concrete all add labour hours. A retrofit in a finished office costs more than cabling during a fit-out.</p><h3>4. SIRA compliance (Dubai)</h3><p>In Dubai, many commercial premises must install a CCTV system that meets Security Industry Regulatory Agency (SIRA) standards, using a SIRA-approved contractor. SIRA-compliant systems have minimum requirements for camera placement, resolution, retention period and recorder security. This raises cost but is mandatory for regulated premises, so confirm whether your business falls under it before accepting a cheaper non-compliant quote. Abu Dhabi and other emirates have their own security authority requirements.</p><h2>Supply-only vs turnkey</h2><p>You can buy hardware yourself and hire an installer separately, but a turnkey quote (supply plus install plus configuration plus warranty) is usually cleaner for accountability. If one part fails, you have a single point of responsibility. For SIRA-regulated sites, turnkey through an approved contractor is the safer route.</p><h2>How to compare CCTV quotes fairly</h2><p>Cheap headline numbers often hide gaps. Before you accept anything, check that each quote states:</p><ul><li>Exact camera model, resolution and brand</li><li>Recorder type, channels and hard drive size (in TB and retention days)</li><li>Whether SIRA compliance is included (if required)</li><li>Cabling material and whether conduit is included</li><li>Warranty period and post-installation support</li><li>Whether mobile/remote viewing setup is included</li></ul><p>The fastest way to compare is to post one clear scope and receive multiple structured quotes side by side. On QuotesDone you can <a href=\"https://quotesdone.com/\">post your CCTV job to verified UAE suppliers</a> anonymously, so you get honest pricing without your details being shared until you choose a supplier. Because suppliers quote blind, they compete on price and spec rather than reputation alone.</p><h2>Ongoing costs to budget for</h2><ul><li><strong>Maintenance:</strong> periodic camera cleaning, firmware updates and checks</li><li><strong>Hard drive replacement:</strong> drives running 24/7 wear out over a few years</li><li><strong>SIRA renewal:</strong> where applicable, systems may need periodic recertification</li></ul><h2>Getting an accurate quote</h2><p>To price a CCTV job well, a supplier needs: your emirate and premises type, number of coverage points, indoor vs outdoor, whether SIRA compliance applies, required retention days, and ideally a floor plan or site photos. The clearer your brief, the tighter your quotes.</p><p>Ready to price your system? <a href=\"https://quotesdone.com/app\">Get anonymous quotes</a> from verified UAE suppliers, compare specs side by side, and pay only a 3% reveal fee on the quote you accept.</p><h2>FAQ</h2><h3>Do I need SIRA approval for CCTV in Dubai?</h3><p>Many commercial premises in Dubai must have a SIRA-compliant system installed by a SIRA-approved contractor. Requirements depend on your business type, so confirm your obligation before buying a non-compliant setup that may need replacing.</p><h3>Is IP CCTV worth the extra cost over analog?</h3><p>For most businesses that need clear footage for identification, evidence or compliance, IP cameras are worth it thanks to higher resolution and better night performance. For a small basic deterrent, HD analog can be enough.</p><h3>How much footage should I store?</h3><p>Thirty days is a common target and is often required for compliance. Longer retention or higher-resolution cameras mean a larger hard drive, so factor storage into the recorder spec when comparing quotes.</p>",
