"how-much-does-epoxy-flooring-cost-in-the-uae-2026-price-guide": {
"slug": "how-much-does-epoxy-flooring-cost-in-the-uae-2026-price-guide",
"title": "How much does epoxy flooring cost in the UAE? (2026 price guide)",
"excerpt": "A 2026 breakdown of epoxy flooring prices in the UAE by coating type, thickness and area, plus what drives cost and how to compare supplier quotes fairly.",
"meta_desc": "Epoxy flooring cost per sqm in the UAE for warehouses, kitchens & showrooms in 2026. Coating types, thickness, prep costs and how to compare quotes.",
"body_html": "<p>Epoxy flooring is one of the most common upgrades for UAE warehouses, workshops, commercial kitchens, car parks and showrooms. It is durable, easy to clean and handles the heat and heavy footfall typical of industrial areas across Dubai, Sharjah and Abu Dhabi. But because \"epoxy\" covers everything from a thin roller coat to a 3mm self-levelling system, prices swing widely. This 2026 guide breaks down realistic rates and what actually drives the number on your quote.</p><h2>Epoxy flooring cost per sqm in the UAE (2026)</h2><p>As an indicative range for supply-and-apply work, expect the following. These are market-observed ranges, not fixed prices, and the final rate depends heavily on area size and floor condition.</p><ul><li><strong>Standard epoxy coating (2 coats, roller):</strong> roughly AED 35-60 per sqm</li><li><strong>Epoxy with primer + broadcast finish (anti-slip):</strong> roughly AED 55-90 per sqm</li><li><strong>Self-levelling epoxy (1.5-3mm):</strong> roughly AED 90-180 per sqm</li><li><strong>Heavy-duty industrial / PU epoxy:</strong> roughly AED 150-280 per sqm</li><li><strong>Epoxy line marking (car parks / warehouses):</strong> roughly AED 8-20 per linear metre</li></ul><p>Larger continuous areas (say 1,000 sqm and above) usually attract a lower per-sqm rate because mobilisation and labour are spread across more coverage. Small or awkward areas cost more per sqm.</p><h3>What is included in a proper epoxy job</h3><ul><li>Surface preparation (grinding or shot blasting)</li><li>Crack and joint repair, plus moisture check</li><li>Primer coat</li><li>Body coat(s) and finish coat</li><li>Curing time and handover cleaning</li></ul><h2>What drives the price up or down</h2><h3>1. Surface preparation</h3><p>This is the biggest hidden cost. Mechanical diamond grinding or shot blasting is essential for adhesion, and if the existing floor is oily, cracked, or has an old coating to remove, prep alone can add AED 15-40 per sqm. A quote that skips proper prep is the most common reason epoxy peels within a year.</p><h3>2. Thickness and system type</h3><p>A thin decorative coat for a showroom is a fraction of the cost of a 3mm self-levelling system for a forklift-heavy warehouse. Match the specification to the load, not just the look.</p><h3>3. Slip resistance and chemical exposure</h3><p>Commercial kitchens and wet areas often need anti-slip broadcast finishes and food-safe or chemical-resistant systems, which cost more than a plain warehouse coat.</p><h3>4. Moisture and slab condition</h3><p>High moisture in the slab requires a moisture-tolerant primer. Skipping this in the UAE's ground-floor slabs is a frequent cause of blistering.</p><h3>5. Area size and access</h3><p>Occupied buildings, night-work requirements and multiple small rooms all push the price up compared with one open, empty slab.</p><h2>Supply-only vs turnkey</h2><p>If you only need materials, an epoxy kit is far cheaper, but application is where most floors fail. For anything beyond a small storeroom, turnkey (materials plus professional grinding and application with a warranty) is the safer buy. When you request quotes, state clearly whether you want supply-only or turnkey so suppliers price the same scope.</p><h2>How to compare epoxy flooring quotes fairly</h2><p>Cheap epoxy quotes usually cut corners on preparation or coat thickness, and the difference only shows up months later. Before you compare numbers, make sure every quote covers the same scope:</p><ul><li>Preparation method stated (grinding vs shot blasting vs acid etch)</li><li>System type and total dry film thickness in mm or microns</li><li>Number of coats and product brand</li><li>Anti-slip finish included or not</li><li>Curing / re-use time before you can walk or drive on it</li><li>Warranty period in writing</li><li>Site condition assumptions (any repair works excluded?)</li></ul><p>A well-structured request gets you comparable numbers instead of a spread you cannot interpret. This is where a marketplace helps: on QuotesDone you post one clear scope and receive multiple quotes from verified UAE suppliers who quote anonymously, so you compare on price and specification rather than on sales pressure. You only pay a 3% reveal fee on the single quote you choose to accept.</p><p>For related surfaces and prep, you may also want to review <a href=\"https://quotesdone.com/\">tiling and marble work</a> if part of your space needs a different finish.</p><p>Ready to price your floor? <a href=\"https://quotesdone.com/app\">Get anonymous quotes</a> from verified epoxy contractors across the UAE.</p><h2>FAQ</h2><h3>How long does epoxy flooring last in the UAE?</h3><p>A properly prepared and applied system typically lasts 5-10 years in commercial use, and longer for heavy-duty systems. Poor surface preparation is the main reason floors fail early, so prioritise prep over the cheapest coating price.</p><h3>Can epoxy be applied over old tiles or existing flooring?</h3><p>Sometimes, but the surface must be mechanically prepared and sound. Loose tiles, oil contamination or high slab moisture must be resolved first. A good contractor will inspect before quoting rather than promising a coat straight over anything.</p><h3>How much downtime should I plan for?</h3><p>Most systems need curing time before foot traffic (often 12-24 hours) and longer before heavy or vehicle loads (typically several days). Ask each supplier to confirm re-use times so you can schedule around your operations.</p>",
